            <ArticleTitle>Acupuncture for pain relief during induced labour in nulliparae: a randomised controlled study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>440-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1471-0528.2010.02825.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the role of acupuncture for analgesia during labour.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Double-blind study of manual, electro and sham acupuncture, and single-blind study comparing acupuncture with a control group for analgesia for labour induction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A major obstetric unit in the UK.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POPULATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cohort of 105 nulliparae undergoing labour induction at term.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-three subjects needed to be randomised to each group to have an 80% power of detecting a 50% relative reduction in epidural rate with an alpha value of 0.05.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The primary end point was the rate of intrapartum epidural analgesia, and the secondary end points were parenteral analgesia requirement, labour length, delivery mode, neonatal condition and postpartum haemorrhage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was no difference in epidural analgesia between acupuncture and sham acupuncture, relative risk 1.18 (95% CI 0.8-1.74), or between acupuncture and control, relative risk 0.88 (95% CI 0.66-1.19). There were no significant differences in the secondary end points between the acupuncture groups and the control group. Side effects or complications of acupuncture were not identified.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Using the protocols studied, there was no analgesic benefit with acupuncture for pain relief during induced labour in nulliparae.</AbstractText>
